<p>Select <code>drama3_codec.safetensors</code> in <strong>Load Drama 3 Codec</strong>, confirm the saved voice is selected in <strong>Load Drama 3 Voice</strong>, edit the text in <strong>Drama 3 Generate</strong>, and queue the workflow. To compare quants, change only the model filename and keep the voice, prompt, seed, and sampling settings fixed.</p>
<p><code>expert_cache_gb = 0</code> enables automatic sizing and was also tested. The cache setting budgets <strong>expert cache VRAM</strong>, not total GPU memory. Dense layers, activations, and temporary buffers need additional space.</p>
<p>To use your own reference, start from the node bundle&#39;s <code>example_workflows/drama3_voice_clone.json</code>. Supply a clean speech clip and its exact transcript to <strong>Drama 3 Encode Voice</strong>, then save it with <strong>Save Drama 3 Voice</strong> for reuse. Qwen3-ASR weights are a separate optional download if you want automatic transcription; the saved-voice test above does not require them.</p>
<p>Example text:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">[sighs] Well, that could have gone better. [laughs] Let&#39;s try again.
</code></pre>
<p>The model also supports speaker tags such as <code>&lt;|speaker:0|&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;|speaker:1|&gt;</code>. Match each supplied voice&#39;s transcript to its speaker tag when using multiple voices; see the upstream node documentation for the full prompt syntax.</p>
