<p>Four English cases—two seeds for a short utterance, narration, and a laughter-tagged utterance—were run twice per model after warmup, for eight timed takes per variant. Execution was serial, with reversed model order in the second round and no ASR running during timing. Each generation began with an empty expert cache. PyTorch&#39;s allocator was capped at 30 GiB.</p>
<p>The table compares complete implementations: K-quants use BF16 activations and Triton packed decode; W4A8 uses Comfy Kitchen&#39;s native path with INT8 activations. Equal cache byte budgets hold different numbers of experts. Q2_K and Q3_K timing ranges overlap, so the small median difference does not establish a consistent speed advantage.</p>
<p>All requested dialogue words were recovered in the first-round ASR check. Q2_K&#39;s laughter-tagged take also transcribed as “Haha,” counted as one insertion. Repeated runs produced identical code sequences within each quant. Both quants passed queued ComfyUI workflows; Q2_K additionally passed a visible UI Run. The focused loader patch was separately exercised with Q2_K, Q3_K, and corrected W4A8 using automatic cache sizing.</p>
<p>These checks establish intelligibility on a small English set. They do not establish equivalent voice similarity, emotion, nonverbal delivery, multilingual pronunciation, or long-form reliability. Weight error was measured over the same 640 expert-bank samples and is not an audio-quality score.</p>
<p><strong>Audio is returned after generation and codec decoding.</strong> First codec frame timing measures generated codes, not first audible audio. Generation real-time factor is generation time divided by output duration and excludes codec decoding. The current ComfyUI workflow does not stream audio. These are not RTX 5090 benchmark results.</p>
